{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rally (new 20–30d highs), the model level is systematically too low across the whole horizon. Apply an additive uplift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–3, larger by days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts (Dec rally). If recent slope is positive and increasing (higher highs + higher lows), increase forecast slope (not just level) so the path keeps rising instead of flattening around ~70–75.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors widen with time. Use a ramp correction: near-term +~0.5–1.5, mid-horizon (days 5–15) +~3–6, long-horizon (days 16–30) +~6–12 during strong bull regimes.",
    "Over-mean-reversion after spikes: after a sharp up-move, the model pulls back too quickly (forecasts dip/flatten) while realized prices stay elevated. Reduce mean-reversion strength for 1–3 weeks after a breakout; keep forecasts closer to the new higher plateau.",
    "Peak/plateau misplacement: the model often places a local peak too early and too low, then declines, whereas the true series continues climbing before peaking. Shift any predicted peak later (by ~5–10 days) and raise the peak level in strong uptrends.",
    "When prices start to roll over (early Jan), the model still remains below realized levels but captures direction better; corrections should be smaller and mainly level-based (avoid adding large positive ramps once the last ~5 days show lower highs)."
  ]
}
